Here's my review of the six tests:

---

## 1. Sleepy Test — `Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes` (line 62)

**Severity: High**
A hard-coded `Thread.Sleep(3000)` makes the test slow _and_ flaky. 3 s is arbitrary: too short on a loaded CI box → false failure; always too long on a fast one → wasted time. This is the single worst problem in the file because it produces non-deterministic results in CI.

**Fix:** Wait on an observable signal with a timeout.

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    await service.ReplenishAsync("SKU-1");

    Assert.True(service.WasReplenished("SKU-1"));
}
```

If `ReplenishAsync` doesn't return an awaitable, wrap the poll with a bounded retry instead of a blind sleep:

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    service.ReplenishAsync("SKU-1");

    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;

    while (!service.WasReplenished("SKU-1"))
    {
        Assert.True(DateTime.UtcNow < deadline,
            "Replenish did not complete within the timeout.");
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }

    Assert.True(service.WasReplenished("SKU-1"));
}
```

---

## 2. Assertion-Free Test — `Audit_RunsWithoutError` (line 75)

**Severity: High**
This test has **zero assertions**. It will pass even if `Audit()` silently corrupts data, returns garbage, or becomes a no-op. A test that can never fail is worse than no test—it gives false confidence. That's why this is high, not medium.

**Fix:** Assert the observable side-effect of an audit.

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Audit_RunsWithoutError()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    var result = service.Audit();

    Assert.NotNull(result);
    Assert.True(result.ItemsAudited > 0);
}
```

_(Adapt the assertions to whatever `Audit()` actually returns or mutates. If it truly has no observable output, the API itself needs rethinking.)_

---

## 3. Bare Skip — `Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities` (line 50)

**Severity: Medium**
`Skip = "skip"` gives no rationale, no tracking issue, no owner. Skipped tests are invisible in dashboards; without a reason this will rot forever. It's medium rather than high because the test _body_ looks correct—if it were re-enabled it would at least verify something. The risk is organizational, not functional.

**Fix:** Either provide a real justification + tracking issue, or delete/re-enable.

```csharp
[Fact(Skip = "Tracked by #2045 - Restock depends on supplier API stub not yet available")]
public void Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();

    service.Restock("SKU-9", 50);

    Assert.Equal(50, service.QuantityOf("SKU-9"));
}
```

If there is no known blocking issue, just remove the `Skip` and let it run.

---

## 4. Reasoned Skip — `Reserve_AcrossWarehouses_BalancesStock` (line 37)

**Severity: Low**
This skip _does_ cite a tracking issue (`#1487`) and explains the dependency (warehouse API redesign). That's the right way to disable a test. The only concern is staleness—if `#1487` was closed months ago this test should have been re-enabled. Low severity because the mechanism is correct; it just needs periodic housekeeping.

**Recommendation:** Verify that issue `#1487` is still open. If resolved, remove the `Skip`:

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Reserve_AcrossWarehouses_BalancesStock()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 10);

    var reserved = service.Reserve("SKU-1", 4);

    Assert.True(reserved);
}
```

---

## Summary

| # | Test | Problem | Severity | Why |
|---|------|---------|----------|-----|
| 1 | `Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes` | `Thread.Sleep(3000)` — flaky & slow | **High** | Non-deterministic failures in CI; wastes wall-clock time every run |
| 2 | `Audit_RunsWithoutError` | No assertions at all | **High** | Can never fail; masks regressions with false confidence |
| 3 | `Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities` | `Skip = "skip"` — no rationale | **Medium** | Will silently rot; risk is organizational, test body is fine |
| 4 | `Reserve_AcrossWarehouses_BalancesStock` | Skipped with a good reason | **Low** | Correctly documented; just confirm `#1487` is still relevant |

`AddItems_ThreeAdded_CountIsThree` and `RemoveItem_FromTwo_LeavesOne` are clean—well-structured Arrange/Act/Assert with contextually obvious literals. No issues.
